  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <section class="py-24 md:py-20 bg-[var(--color-cream)]">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="text-center mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display text-[var(--color-charcoal)] mb-6">
          A-Frame FAQ
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <details class="group bg-white p-6">
          <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer list-none">
            <h3 class="font-display text-xl text-[var(--color-charcoal)] pr-8">How much does it cost to build an A-frame cabin in Ontario?</h3>
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-[var(--color-timber)] transform group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4"></path>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-slate)] leading-relaxed">
            <p>A-frame construction costs run $220 to $300 per square foot in Ontario—20-40% less than traditional builds. This is the construction cost and does not include the A-frame kit package (kits typically start around $60K+). An 800 sq ft seasonal cabin runs $175,000-$200,000 for construction. A year-round 1,200 sq ft home with premium finishes is typically $315,000-$400,000. The range depends on site access, foundation type, finishes, and whether you need well/septic. We provide fixed-price quotes after seeing your lot.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group bg-white p-6">
          <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer list-none">
            <h3 class="font-display text-xl text-[var(--color-charcoal)] pr-8">Are A-frame houses cheaper to build than traditional homes?</h3>
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-[var(--color-timber)] transform group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4"></path>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-slate)] leading-relaxed">
            <p>Yes. A-frames typically cost 20-40% less than conventional homes of similar square footage. The savings come from simpler framing (30-50% less framing time), reduced material requirements, and faster construction. However, per usable square foot, the savings are smaller because A-frames lose floor space to the angled walls on upper levels.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group bg-white p-6">
          <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer list-none">
            <h3 class="font-display text-xl text-[var(--color-charcoal)] pr-8">How long does it take to build an A-frame?</h3>
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-[var(--color-timber)] transform group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4"></path>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-slate)] leading-relaxed">
            <p>Most A-frames take 3-5 months from foundation pour to move-in—compared to 6-12 months for traditional builds. Permits add 4-8 weeks before construction starts, depending on the municipality. Remote locations with challenging access take longer. We give realistic timelines in our initial consultation.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group bg-white p-6">
          <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer list-none">
            <h3 class="font-display text-xl text-[var(--color-charcoal)] pr-8">Can an A-frame cabin be a year-round home in Ontario?</h3>
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-[var(--color-timber)] transform group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4"></path>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-slate)] leading-relaxed">
            <p>Absolutely. We build A-frames to R-40+ insulation standards with modern HVAC, triple-pane windows, and proper air sealing. They're as warm and comfortable as any custom home. Many of our clients live in their A-frames full-time through Ontario winters. The key is building it right from the start—retrofit insulation is expensive and never as effective.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group bg-white p-6">
          <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer list-none">
            <h3 class="font-display text-xl text-[var(--color-charcoal)] pr-8">What are the disadvantages of A-frame houses?</h3>
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-[var(--color-timber)] transform group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4"></path>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-slate)] leading-relaxed">
            <p>The main drawbacks are: 1) Limited storage space due to angled walls (built-ins help), 2) Reduced usable floor space on upper levels, 3) Furniture placement requires planning, 4) Large windows can mean privacy concerns without proper coverings, 5) Heat rises making upper lofts hot (solved with proper HVAC design), 6) Roof repairs are more challenging due to steep pitch. A-frames work best for couples, small families, or anyone who values dramatic design over maximum storage.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group bg-white p-6">
          <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer list-none">
            <h3 class="font-display text-xl text-[var(--color-charcoal)] pr-8">Do A-frames need special permits in Ontario?</h3>
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-[var(--color-timber)] transform group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4"></path>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-slate)] leading-relaxed">
            <p>A-frames require the same building permits as any other home in Ontario—a building permit from the municipality, plus septic and conservation authority approvals depending on your lot. Some townships have specific zoning requirements for roof pitch or building height. We handle all permit applications and know what each municipality requires.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group bg-white p-6">
          <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer list-none">
            <h3 class="font-display text-xl text-[var(--color-charcoal)] pr-8">What size A-frame can I build?</h3>
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-[var(--color-timber)] transform group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4"></path>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-slate)] leading-relaxed">
            <p>A-frames range from compact 400 sq ft cabins to spacious 2,500+ sq ft family homes. Most of our builds fall between 800 and 1,800 sq ft. Larger A-frames often incorporate dormers, bump-outs, or a traditional second floor section to maximize usable space. We help you figure out what size makes sense for your needs and lot.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group bg-white p-6">
          <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer list-none">
            <h3 class="font-display text-xl text-[var(--color-charcoal)] pr-8">Where do you build A-frames in Ontario?</h3>
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-[var(--color-timber)] transform group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4"></path>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-slate)] leading-relaxed">
            <p>We build throughout Muskoka (Huntsville, Bracebridge, Gravenhurst, Port Carling), Kawartha Lakes (Lindsay, Bobcaygeon, Haliburton), Durham Region, York Region, and the GTA. Most of our A-frame projects are in cottage country, but we've also built A-frames as primary residences closer to the city. If your lot is outside these areas, ask. We go further afield when the project needs it.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

